A||||7||JohnnyK||Front ball joints||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-04-2000||06:35 PM||||I am taking the front upper a-arm out of the car to get new bushings pressed into it, and am wondering about the ball joint that I have to split to remove it.  I have one of the "pickle forks" here, and have read that you can use that to remove it, but it will damage the ball joint and make it unusable? Is there a way that I can reuse the joint after splitting it? Also, in some other threads I looked at, they said you needed a ball joint press or to take it to a shop to get them put back on. Do you mean to get the actual joints pressed into the a-arm, or to get the ball joint back together (hopefully not just to get the ball joint together).. If not, how would I connect them once I am done? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-04-2000||07:14 PM||||1) A pickle fork will often damage the rubber grease boot, plus it can put a strain on the ball joint and possibly could cause damage.<P>2) The other option is to use a special ball joint press, which "splits" the ball joint off the spindle. This is the best way to do it if you're planning on reusing the parts.<P>3) You might be confusing the upper with the lower ball joint. The lower ones are pressed onto the control arm and often require a shop with a big hydraulic press. The upper ball joints are bolted on and are much easier to replace. If the upper ball joints are removed or replace from the upper control arm, then you'll have to have an alignment done.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||07-04-2000||08:53 PM||||Under the ball joint, there is a cotter pin through a nut.. I'm actually wondering if I took that nut off, could I just take that whole assembler to the shop for them to press? (Undo that cotter-pin nut, and then the long nut going through the top of the a-arm and the bushings?)||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-04-2000||10:26 PM||||That nut with the cotter pin is where you have to take the ball joint loose from the spindle. You don't want to take the whole assembly loose. There's a coil spring that holds a lot of energy to think about. The easiest way to do this is to put a jack stand under the lower control arm to support it and the spindle, separate the ball joint from the spindle with a pickle fork. Replace the ball joint (it's probably has some wear on it anyway) with Moog slotted ball joints that allow more alignment adjustment. Pull the control arm and change bushings. I replaced or upgraded everything that could possibly wear out on the suspension of my car and it's like a new Fiero now...well worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||07-04-2000||10:40 PM||||Seems to me that would be alot easier though, if I put some wood under the arm so it doesn't fly somewhere (unless it springs up) and undo that nut, and then pop it off? Hayes says to undo that nut to split the ball joint.. Is that necessary?||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-05-2000||12:00 AM||||"Split" the ball joint is just an expression for taking it loose at the spindle. The threaded shaft that the cotter pin is on is tapered. This combined with a little rust can make them stubborn at times. When you hammer a pickle fork between the spindle and ball joint, it'll will pop or "split" loose. It really isn't rocket science. Jack up the car, pull the wheels off and look at how it's all put together.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||07-05-2000||01:54 AM||||I thought splitting it meant splitting the actual joint (Where the ball goes into the shaft i'm assuming), or can that not be done? I think i'm getting somewhere if you cannot do that, and just have to take it all off...||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||07-05-2000||09:33 AM||||Ball joints are a sealed unit. If you get them apart, then it's broken and can't be put back together. My advice is still to replace the ball joints with new ones. Cost is only about $30 each, I think. Cheap insurance.||||reg||1
